{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_7","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"mcporter call exa.web_search_exa query='Intervals.icu October 2026 workout export Garmin heart rate range workout_doc target format' numResults=5\"","aggregated_output":"Title: API access to Intervals.icu\nURL: https://forum.intervals.icu/t/api-access-to-intervals-icu/609\nPublished: N/A\nAuthor: N/A\nHighlights:\nUpdate 9th October 2026: Garmin terms and conditions have been updated and It is likely that we will have to block access to Garmin data via the API soon.\n\nUpdate 14th October 2026: The revisions to the agreement are part of the planned reopening of the Garmin Connect Developer Program. There will be an easier path for individuals and apps to access data from Garmin devices.\n\n...\n\nUpdate 22 April 2021:\n\n- The workout description is now parsed. This means that workouts created or updated via the API have training load calculated and get “time in zones” and so on. This also applies to workouts synced from an external calendar.\n- You can now create/update workouts from zwo, mrc and erg files.\n\n`POST /api/v1/athlete/{id}/events`\n\n```auto\n{\n  \"category\": \"WORKOUT\",\n  \"start_date_local\": \"2021-04-29T00:00:00\",\n  \"type\": \"Ride\",\n  \"filename\": \"4x8m.zwo\",\n  \"file_contents\": \"<?xml version=\\\"1.0\\\" encoding=\\\"UTF-8\\\" standalone=\\\"yes\\\"?>\\n<workout_file>...\"\n}\n\n...\n\nResponse:\n\n```auto\n{\n  \"id\": 610062,\n  \"icu_training_load\": 116,\n  ...\n  \"name\": \"4x8m VO2 Max\",\n  \"description\": \"These are horribly nasty.\\nCategory: Horrible\\n\\n- 20m 60% 90-100rpm\\n\\nMain set 4x\\n- 8m 110%\\n- 8m 50%\\n\\n- 10m 60%\\n\",\n  ...\n  \"workout_doc\": {\n    \"steps\": [\n      {\n        \"power\": {\"units\": \"%ftp\", \"value\": 60},\n        \"cadence\": {\"end\": 100, \"start\": 90, \"units\": \"rpm\"}, \"duration\": 1200\n      }, ...\n    ],\n    \"duration\": 5640,\n    \"zoneTimes\": [1920, 1800, 0, 0, 1920, 0, 0, 0],\n    \"hrZoneTimes\": [1920, 1800, 0, 0, 1920, 0, 0, 0]\n  },\n  \"icu_intensity\": 86.04798\n}\n\n...\n\nThere is a catch: Workouts have a “workout_doc” field which is the parsed workout steps. Currently the parser is only available client side. So before you can download a workout in a different format or have it sync to Garmin Connect you need to edit it using the Intervals.icu app so the workout_doc field is populated.\n\n---\n\nTitle: How to send structured workouts with repeats and pace/power ranges via API?\nURL: https://forum.intervals.icu/t/how-to-send-structured-workouts-with-repeats-and-pace-power-ranges-via-api/130971\nPublished: N/A\nAuthor: N/A\nHighlights:\nThe `workout_doc` looks correct. However, when I open the workout on my Garmin, I only see the duration. The pace range is missing. Is the `pace` field in `workout_doc` supposed to be converted to a pace target when the workout is pushed to Garmin? The same problem when upload a FIT file.\n\n...\n\n1) Basic line format\n\n...\n\nThat’s intentional. Both Pace and Power targets are sent as a % of threshold. This makes workouts portable over longer time (where threshold changes) and in between athletes with different thresholds.\nSo you need threshold and zones to be configured.\n\n...\n\nYou can use absolute pace and power values but for the reasons above, it is discouraged.\nYou need to set threshold and zones in Intervals to get correct load and full analysis. No way around that.\n\nI don’t know exactly how absolute Pace is handled but for absolute Power (on Garmin - not every brand), you have to send a value over 1000. Any value below 1000 is interpreted as a percentage of threshold. For values above 1000, the first digit (the 1) is dropped and the rest is used as absolute power target value.\n\n- 50 = 50%\n- 100 = 100%\n- 400 = 400%\n- 1050 = 50W\n- 1100 = 100W\n- 11100 = 1100W\n\nBut without threshold and zones, Intervals can’t attribute a Load to a workout with absolute values, so it won’t work.\n\n---\n\nTitle: Pace targets lost in Garmin export for API-created running workouts — steps arrive on watch as \"No Target\" (parsed correctly in workout_doc) - Intervals.icu Forum\nURL: https://forum.intervals.icu/t/pace-targets-lost-in-garmin-export-for-api-created-running-workouts-steps-arrive-on-watch-as-no-target-parsed-correctly-in-workout-doc/130706\nPublished: 2026-07-17T00:00:00.000Z\nAuthor: N/A\nHighlights:\nPace targets on running workout steps are being lost somewhere in the Intervals.icu → Garmin Connect export path. We’ve verified every link we can reach, and the data is correct on the Intervals.icu side — this looks like an export issue, not a syntax issue.\n\nSetup\n\n- Workouts created via the API: `POST /api/v1/athlete/0/events/bulk?upsert=true`, `category: WORKOUT`, `type: Run`, description in the plain-text workout format, `external_id` set\n- Athlete settings: `garmin_pace_range = 2.5` (default), Garmin Connect linked with “Upload planned workouts” enabled\n- Watch: Garmin Forerunner [model 165] — happy to provide exact model/firmware\n\nDescription format used (sample):\n\n```auto\n- 2.5km 5:57-5:23/km Pace\n\n```\n\n`- 1.5km 9:06-8:14/km Pace`\n\n`- 2km 6:13-5:37/km Pace`\n\nWhat works (verified via the API):\n\nReading the event back, `workout_doc.steps` is parsed perfectly — every step carries a structured pace range:\n\n```auto\n{\"pace\":{\"start\":357,\"end\":323,\"units\":\"secs/km\"},\"distance\":2500,\"duration\":848}\n\n```\n\n21/21 steps have `pace: {start, end}` on the real workout in question.\n\nWhat fails:\n\nThe workout reaches Garmin Connect (name + description arrive; the Connect app shows the description). On the watch, the steps arrive with correct distances but every step shows “No Target” — no pace target, no alerts during the workout.\n\nWhat we’ve ruled out:\n\n- Syntax: both single-value (`5:40/km Pace`) and range (`5:45-5:35/km Pace`) formats parse into correct `workout_doc` pace objects; both arrive on the watch as “No Target”\n- Stale Garmin cache: deleting the events and re-creating them fresh (to force a full re-export rather than an update) changes nothing\n- Connect app display quirk (the `[SOLVED]` thread #87358): this is on the watch itself , in the step list and during execution, not just the phone UI\n\n...\n\nSolved — and the cause was on our end, not intervals’. The athlete simply had no Run threshold pace set in their sport settings.\n\nWith threshold_pace unset, intervals drops the per-step pace targets from the Garmin export (it falls back to HR), so every step lands as “No Target” on the watch — even though workout_doc.steps carried correct pace ranges on the intervals side the whole time. HR-target workouts were unaffected because LTHR was set, which is why only pace workouts broke.\n\nFix: set a Run threshold pace — Settings → sport settings, or via API: PUT /api/v1/athlete/0/sport-settings/{runGroupId} with body { “threshold_pace”: \\ }. intervals then auto-populates the pace zones, and after a fresh push the pace targets appear on the watch (confirmed on a Garmin Forerunner).\n\nOne gotcha for anyone re-testing: existing events need a delete + recreate to re-trigger the Garmin export — an in-place update alone doesn’t.\n\nBig thanks to R2Tom and MedTechCD — the “Did you setup your Pace zones?” pointer in the related threads was exactly it.\n\n---\n\nTitle: Downloading planned workouts from the API\nURL: https://forum.intervals.icu/t/downloading-planned-workouts-from-the-api/93737\nPublished: N/A\nAuthor: N/A\nHighlights:\nYou can download planned workouts from an athlete’s calendar in zwo (Zwift), fit, mrc and erg format (CALENDAR:READ scope required):\n\n```auto\n$ curl 'https://intervals.icu/api/v1/athlete/0/events?category=WORKOUT&ext=zwo&resolve=true' \\\n    -H 'Authorization: Bearer d842c1fc25f241e5ae440d09756448a9'\n\n```\n\nThe optional category parameter specifies what sort of events to return (planned workouts in this case). The ext parameter the format (leave out if you don’t need the file). The resolve=true parameter converts power (and heart rate etc.) from units like % of FTP into watts. The default date range is a week from today (use oldest and newest parameters in ISO-8601 format to change).\n\nExample abridged response:\n\n```auto\n[\n  {\n    \"id\": 46696127,\n    \"start_date_local\": \"2024-11-21T07:00:00\",\n    \"type\": \"Ride\",\n    \"category\": \"WORKOUT\",\n    \"name\": \"Big Gear Grinds\",\n    \"description\": \"...\",\n    ...\n    \"workout_doc\": {\n      \"description\": \"A great session for building cycling specific strength both in and out of the saddle.\",\n      \"target\": \"POWER\",\n      \"steps\": [\n        {\n          \"text\": \"Welcome to the ...\",\n          \"duration\": 180,\n          \"warmup\": true,\n          \"ramp\": true,\n          \"power\": { \"start\": 35.0, \"end\": 55.0, \"units\": \"%ftp\" },\n          \"cadence\": { \"value\": 85.0, \"units\": \"rpm\" },\n          \"_power\": { \"value\": 126.0, \"start\": 98.0, \"end\": 154.0 }\n        },\n      ],\n      ...\n    },\n    ...\n    \"workout_filename\": \"Big_Gear_Grinds.zwo\",\n    \"workout_file_base64\": \"PD94bWwgdmVy...\"\n  }\n]\n\n```\n\nThe workout_file_base64 field contains the file in the specified format (zwo in this case) encoded as base 64 text.\n\nThe workout_doc field contains the workout in native Intervals.icu format. If you don’t already have a parser for one of the other formats then this might be easier to work with.\n\nThis is how the workout_doc field is mapped in Intervals.icu:\n\n```java\npublic class WorkoutDoc {\n\n    public String description;\n    public Map<String, String> description_locale;\n    public Integer duration;\n    public Float distance;\n    public Integer ftp;\n    public Integer lthr;\n    public Float threshold_pace; // meters/sec\n    public String pace_units; // SECS_100M, SECS_100Y, MINS_KM, MINS_MILE, SECS_500M\n    public SportSettings sportSettings;\n    public String category;\n    public WorkoutTarget target;\n    public List<Step> steps;\n    public List<Object> zoneTimes; // sometimes array of ints otherwise array of objects\n    public Map<String, String> options;\n    public List<String> locales;\n\n    public enum Option {\n        category, pool_length, power\n    }\n\n    public static class Step {\n        public String text;\n        public Map<String, String> text_locale;\n        public Integer duration;\n        public Float distance;\n        public Boolean until_lap_press;\n        public Integer reps;\n        public Boolean warmup;\n        public Boolean cooldown;\n        public String intensity; // \"active\", \"rest\", \"warmup\", \"cooldown\", \"recovery\", \"interval\", \"other\"\n        public List<Step> steps;\n        public Boolean ramp;\n        public Boolean freeride;\n        public Boolean maxeffort;\n        public Value power;\n        public Value hr;\n        public Value pace;\n        public Value cadence;\n        public Boolean hidepower;\n\n        // these are filled in with actual watts, bpm etc. when resolve=true parameter is supplied to the endpoint\n        public Value _power;\n        public Value _hr;\n        public Value _pace;\n        public Float _distance;\n    }\n\n    public static class Value {\n        public Double value;\n        public Double start;\n        public Double end;\n        public String units;\n        public String target;\n        public Integer mmp_duration;\n    }\n}\n\n/** How should workouts be performed? Target power, HR or pace? */\npublic enum WorkoutTarget {\n    AUTO(\"A\"), POWER(\"W\"), HR(\"H\"), PACE(\"P\");\n}\n\n```\n\n---\n\nTitle: Exact HR corridors on Garmin from Intervals.icu API workouts — start/end, not value/end — Worklore\nURL: https://worklore.dev/s/2026-05-12-exact-hr-corridors-on-garmin-from-intervals-icu-api-workouts\nPublished: 2026-05-12T09:54:16.000Z\nAuthor: N/A\nHighlights:\n1. `{units: \"bpm\", value, end}` — API accepted; watch showed 214–249 bpm (impossible). FIT export misread `bpm` as percent-of-something.\n2. `{start_bpm, end_bpm}` — watch showed a constant 101, Garmin's \"no valid HR target\" fallback. Schema silently dropped.\n3. Surveyed the Intervals UI target dropdown, concluded \"absolute bpm is impossible\". Wrong — that dropdown picks the units label, not the value shape.\n\nThe breakthrough: build a workout in the Intervals UI with its \"Instant HR\" toggle, then read it back via the API. The corridor's lower bound is `start`, not `value`. That one key name switches the FIT export mode: `{value, end}` routes as a Lap-HR / zone-mapped target (the watch clamps it to its local zones), while `{start, end}` becomes an Instant-HR corridor — the watch displays the raw bpm Intervals computed. Verified end-to-end: Intervals LTHR 172, watch LTHR 168, a 75–82% target rendered 129–141 bpm on the watch — matching Intervals, drift-immune. The fix was a one-word rename after days of probing.\n\nBonus trap from the same session: steps were invisible in the Intervals web UI, because the UI reads a separate top-level `description` in Intervals' text grammar — where `m` means minutes (`200m` = a 200-minute step!), distance must be `mtr`/`km`, a bare `Z2` resolves to power zones even for swims, and a digit at the start of a label gets eaten as a duration. If the parser chokes, Intervals wipes both representations.\n\n...\n\nSteps:\n\n1. Create an event via `POST /api/v1/athlete/{id}/events` with `workout_doc.steps`, where every HR step target is `{\"units\": \"%lthr\", \"start\":, \"end\":}` — never `value`. Percentages, not absolute bpm: Intervals converts at FIT-export time from its own stored threshold.\n2. Set the event's top-level `description` in Intervals' native text grammar so steps render in the web UI, e.g. `- Warm-up easy 10m 75-82% LTHR`. Rules: durations `m`/`s`, distance `mtr`/`km`, blank lines around `Nx` repeat blocks, no digits at the start of labels, no bare `Z ` outside cycling.\n3. Read the event back with `GET /events/{id}`: non-empty `workout_doc.steps` means the parser accepted the description; `steps: []` means it choked.\n4. Sync the watch through Garmin Connect and open the scheduled workout.\n\nVerify: the watch shows a bpm corridor equal to your percentages × the LTHR stored in Intervals — not the watch's own LTHR (easiest to prove when the two differ). The Intervals web UI shows the full step chart instead of just a name and total duration.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_8","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"mcporter call exa.web_search_exa query='site:forum.intervals.icu October 2026 Garmin instant HR workout export range start end' numResults=8\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_8","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"mcporter call exa.web_search_exa query='site:forum.intervals.icu October 2026 Garmin instant HR workout export range start end' numResults=8\"","aggregated_output":"Title: Power and HR targets for Garmin workouts\nURL: https://forum.intervals.icu/t/power-and-hr-targets-for-garmin-workouts/2926\nPublished: N/A\nAuthor: N/A\nHighlights:\nYou can now specify power and HR targets for workout steps (default is lap power and lap HR):\n\nThis sorts out the issue with HR steps not working on Garmin watches (well at least some of them). You need to choose “Instant HR” (hr=1s in the text):\n\n- warmup 30s 50% LTHR hr=1s\n- Wibble 20m 78% LTHR hr=1s\n- cooldown 30s 40% LTHR hr=1s\n\n...\n\n@david Not sure if anyone else has been successful, but I finally managed to create a structured workout with my Stryd power targets and did it this morning. Below the syntax used, basically the instructions on Garmin during the execution (audio and on watch) were good enough for me to follow the structure. But some tweaks still needed:\n-When displaying the workout on Garmin watch (going in calendar, selecting the workout, then display/preview), for each split I have “Power 3s” prefix and then the lower target level set, instead of the specific power range defined, like I have when I configure a workout from Stryd and push it to Garmin\n-When uploading the FIT back to Intervals, it has associated correctly the activity to the planned workout… But the intervals, although well synchronized (I had some “Press Lap” splits) the type of intervals are not correct. How to properly force specific intervals to be detected as “Work” and others as “Recovery”? Actually same issue in Garmin Connect when looking at the activity, everything but the cooldown at the end is displayed as Work.\n\nBelow the syntax of the workout (sorry for the french text in-line) - note that I used Google Gemini to get some help to write the structure, but had to iterate a couple of times until it was displaying properly and also looking good on the watch when pushing to Garmin):\n\n```auto\nWarmup\n- Course facile 12m 70-80% FTP\n\nActivation 2x\n- Activation 1m 97-103% FTP\n- Récup 2m 70-80% FTP\n\nTransition\n- Transition 2m 70-80% FTP\n\nMain Set 5x\n- 15s 115-130% FTP\n- Descente 15s Press Lap 65-80% FTP\n- 20s 115-130% FTP\n- Descente 20s Press Lap 65-80% FTP\n- 30s 115-130% FTP\n- Descente 30s Press Lap 65-80% FTP\n- 20s 115-130% FTP\n- Descente 20s Press Lap 65-80% FTP\n- 15s 115-130% FTP\n- Descente 15s Press Lap 65-80% FTP\n\nCooldown\n- Retour au calme 10m 65-80% FTP\n\n```\n\n---\n\nTitle: HR target missing from Garmin Connect workout upload\nURL: https://forum.intervals.icu/t/hr-target-missing-from-garmin-connect-workout-upload/5483\nPublished: N/A\nAuthor: N/A\nHighlights:\n> @Upload planned workouts to Garmin Connect:\n> \n> Did you use “Instant HR”? - 30m 80% HR hr=1s [Screen Shot 2021-12-08 at 18.09.45]\n\n...\n\nLook for the “hr=1s” on steps. It doesn’t look like your workouts have that.\n\n...\n\nIt’s not a bug and it’s not a workaround. The screen tells you that you have to choose Instant HR for Garmin watch.\nBut there are people using something else then Garmin watch… The other settings are for head units, Edge for example.\nI’m not sure, but possibly it is enough to make the choice ones to make it default on your account.\n\n...\n\nGarmin Connect knows what device you have.\nYou don’t have to “type” ‘hr=1s’. You can but you can also choose the Instant HR option in the workout builder.\nDid you search the forum for both terms “Instant HR” and “Lap HR”?\nThat might explain quite a bit.\n\n...\n\nPersonally, I have never asked myself the problem. I’ve always gone with instant HR, it seemed to me to be the best solution and it always satisfied me. Rather, I would find it more useful to add a range of Hr min and Hr max to keep, as for watts. For some athletes I take the test for DFA a1 and determined the aerobic threshold that cannot be determined in % on HR is so personal that I would like to set the target manually. For the rest, I think what’s there is great.\n\n---\n\nTitle: [SOLVED] Write min and max heart rate in workout - Intervals.icu Forum\nURL: https://forum.intervals.icu/t/solved-write-min-and-max-heart-rate-in-workout/63267\nPublished: 2024-03-21T00:53:34.000Z\nAuthor: Nik_Om_Cinderella_Ri\nHighlights:\nWhat exactly do you mean?\n\nIn a workout for HR, you use eg\n\n- 1m 100% HR\n\nOr you mean as a comment / Cue?\n\n- full Gaz HR time 1m 100% HR\n\n Gerald 21 March 2024 01:39 3 \n\nI would think as a range:\n\n- 5m 80-85% HR\n\n treesner 21 March 2024 22:24 4 \n\nare beat per minutes supported, garmin allows you to set this up in their work. would something like this work?\n\n- 5m 125-141BPM HR\n\n app4g 21 March 2024 22:29 5 \n\nnope… BPM is not supported Only % HR and Zx HR (i too only remember my HR BPM numbers I want to hit and not zones or %, but it is what it is)\n\n---\n\nTitle: Syncing Pace & HR targets to Garmin - Intervals.icu Forum\nURL: https://forum.intervals.icu/t/syncing-pace-hr-targets-to-garmin/130238\nPublished: 2026-06-01T00:00:00.000Z\nAuthor: N/A\nHighlights:\nNew to intervals.icu and trying to understand the workout syntax a bit better. I’m using my own dev tool to push my plan to intervals.icu with the API, that then syncs it to my Garmin watch. But having a bit of trouble getting the pace targets to sync. Garmin shows ‘No targets’ for my repeats. Currently, this is how my example run looks like\n\n```auto\n- 22m Z2 HR\n\n4x\n- Strides 20s Z4-Z5 Pace\n- Walk 60s\n\n```\n\nInterestingly, removing the first 22mins and just having the whole workout as 4x repeats somehow seems to be working. Any help with this would be appreciated 🙂\n\n...\n\nIntervals hasn’t figured out yet how to send workouts over the Garmin API with multiple targets.\nLet’s be clear: Intervals is NOT sending FIT workouts to Garmin because Garmin doesn’t allow that. Intervals sends a workout in a script format to the Garmin API and Garmin Connect translates that to a workout on your device. The target type definition is not clear or Garmin is somehow blocking that.\nThis is kind of a hot topic over the last couple of months but the solution hasn’t been found yet.\n\n---\n\nTitle: Workout builder - Page 24 - Guide - Intervals.icu Forum\nURL: https://forum.intervals.icu/t/workout-builder/1163?page=24\nPublished: 2020-06-16T11:49:37.000Z\nAuthor: Howie\nHighlights:\nBut the ramp thing… Using the workout cues to “inform” the user would be a good alternative but it would be grossly different from the graphic tho..\n\n...\n\nSure I will send you some files. For both Garmin and Suunto you supply a JSON doc.\n\n...\n\nNo the format is quite different. But all the worked out power/bpm ranges in the JSON download will help.\n\n app4g 17 April 2022 14:32 473 \n\n@david the HR ramps need some loving. but the Power one is fixed.\n\nIn my post here Building workout using zone number instead of percentage range - #24 by app4g,\n\nScreenshot 2022-04-17 at 10.35.43 PM 1216×1270 188 KB\n\nIt’s from this Test workout \n\nScreenshot 2022-04-17 at 10.28.44 PM 1432×778 50 KB\n\n```auto\n[\n  {\n    \"duration\": 300,\n    \"ramp\": true,\n    \"hr\": {\n      \"start\": 100,\n      \"end\": 50,\n      \"units\": \"%hr\"\n    },\n    \"_hr\": {\n      \"value\": 174,\n      \"start\": 116,\n      \"end\": 232\n    }\n  },\n  {\n    \"duration\": 300,\n    \"ramp\": true,\n    \"hr\": {\n      \"start\": 50,\n      \"end\": 100,\n      \"units\": \"%hr\"\n    },\n    \"_hr\": {\n      \"value\": 174,\n      \"start\": 116,\n      \"end\": 232\n    }\n  }\n]\n\n```\n\nGood news now is that the workout shown on Intervals will be similar as workout shown in BreakAway. HR Ramps will be plotted as a Ramp, but internally it’s a range. HR Range will be plotted as a flat line (ave of range) but internally will still be a range)\n\nHR Target Based Training feature, using workouts sync’ed from intervals.icu is finally nearly there.\n\n...\n\ndavid 18 April 2022 12:57 475 \n\nFixed. Ramps are now kept for HR and pace. Will deploy Tues AM (GMT+2).\n\n...\n\neg: Z1 = 100-120 Z2 = 121-140 Z3 = 141-160\n\nso ramp Z1 -Z2 then Z2-Z3 should be 100-120 → 121-140 → 141-160 or will it be middle of the HR Zone?\n\n...\n\nThe zone ranges go from the start of the zone to the end of the zone. So if you to “Z2-Z3” its lowest bpm (or watts) in Z2 to highest in Z3. Just using “Z2” is lowest in Z2 to highest in Z2.\n\nZ1 is a special case with no lower bound so 80% of the upper bound is used.\n\n...\n\nYou can’t do a down ramp down using a single zone specifier.\n\nIt is behaving as expected for the up ramps. The first part of the range (Z2-Z3) uses the lower end of Z2 but the end of the range uses the upper end of Z3. So the next range starting on Z3 will not line up with the end of the first.\n\n---\n\nTitle: Syncing both pace and heart rate to Garmin Connect workout\nURL: https://forum.intervals.icu/t/syncing-both-pace-and-heart-rate-to-garmin-connect-workout/67330\nPublished: N/A\nAuthor: N/A\nHighlights:\nJust to be clear, if I choose “HR” when importing the .fit workout in Intervals, my Garmin watch will not display the correct pace for my 400 meter intervals. It just says, “400m” for the run portion instead of 400m: 4:25 min/km - 4:40 min/km.\n\nHowever, if I directly add the .fit file to my Garmin watch (by connecting it via USB to my computer), I do have both pace and HR data for each workout step, so I think the .fit file is correctly formatted.\n\n...\n\nI encountered this issue as well. If a workout only consists of HR or Pace targets then the output from intervals.icu syncs as expected - any combination thereafter and only a single target type is generated.\n\nFrom intervals.icu I exported a fit file and using (FIT file viewer – RUNALYZE) I could see the HR targets were correct but pace was left as “open”.\n\nI then tried to reproduce a fit file on intervals.icu that was originally created on Garmin Connect:\n\n1. create simple workout with HR target steps and Pace target steps\n2. sync to watch\n3. connect watch to computer and grab the *_workout.fit file\n4. import the fit file to intervals.icu (the plain text steps appear as expected)\n5. export and sync the fit file generated by intervals.icu\n\nIn (5) above the fit file produced does not contain the correct target type information (File Types | FIT SDK | Garmin Developers). Details of the simple workout are listed below.\n\n...\n\n## Note:\n\nThe default workout order on the settings page (“Power, HR, Pace”) does not correctly handle this. Changing it to (“Pace, HR, Power”) will result in pace but then HR targets are “open” in the generated fit file. Ideally, the plain text would prefer the first target type listed so one could properly create mixed target type workouts.\n\n...\n\nWorkout plain text:\n\n```md\n- Warmup 5m Z1 HR\n- 15m Z2 HR\n- 5m 89-94% Pace\n\n```\n\n...\n\nI’ve also seen this on a Garmin 970 for this workout.\n\nWarmup\n\n- Easy w/up 10m Z1 HR\n\nMain\n\n- Relaxed 26m Z2 HR\n\nStrides 4x\n\n- Stride 20s 04:00/km Pace\n- Easy 1m Z1 HR\n\nRecovery\n\n- Cooldown 3m Z1HR\n\nWhat I’ve found is that if I select “Pace” then the pace intervals come through perfectly, but if I set HR, then the HR intervals come through perfectly.\n\nI can create the workout directly in Garmin with both Pace, HR (AND Pwr which I use in hilly areas), so the watch supports it. Not sure whether this is a Garmin API bug or an Intervals.icu export bug.\n\nWatching and happy to troubleshoot. (Or even wade into the code if that’s about anywhere)\n\n...\n\nThis is not supported yet by intervals.\nI think best would be to create a Feature Request Thread.\n\n...\n\nSupport mixed Pace / HR / PWR Garmin workout sync for the request / you can vote for this request !\n\n---\n\nTitle: FIT workout import silently drops REPS steps and mis-scales DISTANCE steps - Bug Reports - Intervals.icu Forum\nURL: https://forum.intervals.icu/t/fit-workout-import-silently-drops-reps-steps-and-mis-scales-distance-steps/131081\nPublished: 2026-08-21T00:00:00.000Z\nAuthor: N/A\nHighlights:\n## Related findings (export side, found while testing a `workout_doc` JSON workaround)\n\nTo work around the above, I tried POSTing `workout_doc` directly in the bulk-events body instead of `file_contents_base64` (bypassing the FIT importer\n). That avoids bugs 1 and 2 above — I confirmed it stores step count/duration correctly and does not inject values it wasn’t given — but surfaced th\nree more issues on your FIT export side (`GET /api/v1/athlete/{id}/events/{id}/download.fit`), all reproduced 2026-08-21 ~02:58 UTC on account `i318 548`:\n\n1. Fabricated HR target on export : a step pushed via FIT import with no target at all (`target_type=OPEN`) came back on the watch/calendar with a\nheart-rate target of 213-242 bpm — a range that exceeds this athlete’s own configured `max_hr` (195). Nothing in the source data suggested a targe\nt at all.\n2. `hr_zone` target exports as a wrong absolute range : a `workout_doc` step with `hr: {value: 2, units: \"hr_zone\"}` (real HR zone 2) exported to F\nIT as `custom_target_heart_rate_low/high = 250/258` — again above this athlete’s max_hr, and nowhere near their real zone-2 boundaries (158-165, per t\nheir own `sportSettings.hr_zones`).\n3. Pace targets silently dropped on export : a `workout_doc` step with a correctly-unit-converted `pace: {start, end, units: \"%pace\"}` target expor\nted with `target_type=OPEN` — the pace target just isn’t encoded into the outbound FIT at all.\n4. 500 on download for an unrecognized `workout_doc` field : a `workout_doc` step with a non-standard key (I tried `\"reps\"`, since there’s apparent\nly no supported field for rep-based strength content) caused `GET .../download.fit` to fail with `{\"status\":500,\"error\":\"Internal server error, trace 8bb19287\"}` instead of ignoring the unknown field or returning a clean 4xx.\n\nI’ve since deleted my test events, so those specific event ids won’t reproduce (2), (3), (4) directly — happy to re-create minimal repro events on req\nuest if that trace id isn’t enough for you to find in your logs, or to share the exact `.fit`/JSON payloads I used.\n\n---\n\nTitle: Garmin Workout Screen - Intervals.icu Forum\nURL: https://forum.intervals.icu/t/garmin-workout-screen/3422\nPublished: 2021-03-23T19:54:25.000Z\nAuthor: Basti_Sternburg\nHighlights:\nThis post explains what you need I think.\n\n> You can now specify power and HR targets for workout steps (default is lap power and lap HR): [Screen Shot 2021-02-14 at 06.57.18] [Screen Shot 2021-02-14 at 06.58.29] This sorts out the issue with HR steps not working on Garmin watches (well at least some of them). You need to choose “Instant HR” (hr=1s in the text): warmup 30s 50% LTHR hr=1s Wibble 20m 78% LTHR hr=1s cooldown 30s 40% LTHR hr=1s [IMG_4164]\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_9","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"mcporter call exa.web_search_exa query='Intervals.icu October 2026 workout export format update HR target Garmin API start end' numResults=10\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_9","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"mcporter call exa.web_search_exa query='Intervals.icu October 2026 workout export format update HR target Garmin API start end' numResults=10\"","aggregated_output":"Title: API access to Intervals.icu\nURL: https://forum.intervals.icu/t/api-access-to-intervals-icu/609\nPublished: N/A\nAuthor: N/A\nHighlights:\nIntervals.icu now has a public API for other applications. To use it you need to generate an API key in /settings (look for “Developer Settings” near the bottom). Your API key allows other applications to access your Intervals.icu data. Be careful what you do with it! If you suspect that your key may have been compromised immediately clear or re-generate it.\n\nBy using the API you agree to the Intervals.icu API Terms and Conditions .\n\nPlease not that data from Strava is not available from the API. Their terms and conditions do not allow it.\n\nUpdate 9th October 2026: Garmin terms and conditions have been updated and It is likely that we will have to block access to Garmin data via the API soon.\n\nUpdate 14th October 2026: The revisions to the agreement are part of the planned reopening of the Garmin Connect Developer Program. There will be an easier path for individuals and apps to access data from Garmin devices.\n\nThe API uses basic authentication for personal use. The username is “API_KEY” and the password your API key. Example using curl:\n\n`$ curl -u API_KEY:1l0nlqjq3j1obdhg08rz5rfhx https://intervals.icu/api/v1/athlete/2049151/activities.csv`\n\nApps intended to be used by more than one person should use OAuth and Bearer tokens.\n\n...\n\n`GET /api/v1/athlete/{id}/events/{eventId}` Fetch an event by id.\n`GET /api/v1/athlete/{id}/events/{eventId}/download{ext}` Download a planned workout in .zwo, .mrc or .erg format.\n`POST /api/v1/athlete/{id}/events` Create event and return it.\n`PUT /api/v1/athlete/{id}/events/{eventId}` Update event and return it.\n`DELETE /api/v1/athlete/{id}/events/{eventId}` Delete the event.\n\nExample POST payload to create a workout (note that the dates cannot have time i.e. must end with `T00:00:00` and the athlete needs to be yourself or someone you are coaching):\n\n```auto\n{\n    \"start_date_local\": \"2020-05-01T00:00:00\",\n    \"icu_training_load\": 120,\n    \"category\": \"WORKOUT\",\n    \"name\": \"Outdoor ride at last!\",\n    \"description\": \"Lockdown finally over, just go ride around all over the place!\",\n    \"type\": \"Ride\",\n    \"moving_time\": 7200\n\n...\n\nResponse:\n\n...\n\nUpdate 22 April 2021:\n\n- The workout description is now parsed. This means that workouts created or updated via the API have training load calculated and get “time in zones” and so on. This also applies to workouts synced from an external calendar.\n- You can now create/update workouts from zwo, mrc and erg files.\n\n`POST /api/v1/athlete/{id}/events`\n\n```auto\n{\n  \"category\": \"WORKOUT\",\n  \"start_date_local\": \"2021-04-29T00:00:00\",\n  \"type\": \"Ride\",\n  \"filename\": \"4x8m.zwo\",\n  \"file_contents\": \"<?xml version=\\\"1.0\\\" encoding=\\\"UTF-8\\\" standalone=\\\"yes\\\"?>\\n<workout_file>...\"\n}\n\n```\n\nResponse:\n\n```auto\n{\n  \"id\": 610062,\n  \"icu_training_load\": 116,\n  ...\n  \"name\": \"4x8m VO2 Max\",\n  \"description\": \"These are horribly nasty.\\nCategory: Horrible\\n\\n- 20m 60% 90-100rpm\\n\\nMain set 4x\\n- 8m 110%\\n- 8m 50%\\n\\n- 10m 60%\\n\",\n  ...\n  \"workout_doc\": {\n    \"steps\": [\n      {\n        \"power\": {\"units\": \"%ftp\", \"value\": 60},\n        \"cadence\": {\"end\": 100, \"start\": 90, \"units\": \"rpm\"}, \"duration\": 1200\n      }, ...\n    ],\n    \"duration\": 5640,\n    \"zoneTimes\": [1920, 1800, 0, 0, 1920, 0, 0, 0],\n    \"hrZoneTimes\": [1920, 1800, 0, 0, 1920, 0, 0, 0]\n  },\n  \"icu_intensity\": 86.04798\n}\n\n...\n\n`GET /api/v1/athlete/{id}/folders` List all folders and workouts\n`POST /api/v1/athlete/{id}/folders` Create a folder\n\n...\n\n`DELETE /api/v1/athlete/{id}/folders/{folderId}` Delete folder\n`GET /api/v1/athlete/{id}/folders/{folderId}/shared-with` Show who folder has been shared with\n\n...\n\n`GET /api/v1/athlete/{id}/workouts` List all workouts (excluding those shared by others)\n`GET /api/v1/athlete/{id}/workouts/{workoutId}` Get a workout\n`POST /api/v1/athlete/{id}/workouts` Create a workout\n\n...\n\n`DELETE\n\n...\n\n`POST /api/v1/download-workout{ext}` Download a workout in .zwo .mrc or .erg format\n\n...\n\nThere is a catch: Workouts have a “workout_doc” field which is the parsed workout steps. Currently the parser is only available client side. So before you can download a workout in a different format or have it sync to Garmin Connect you need to edit it using the Intervals.icu app so the workout_doc field is populated.\n\n---\n\nTitle: Exact HR corridors on Garmin from Intervals.icu API workouts — start/end, not value/end — Worklore\nURL: https://worklore.dev/s/2026-05-12-exact-hr-corridors-on-garmin-from-intervals-icu-api-workouts\nPublished: 2026-05-12T09:54:16.000Z\nAuthor: N/A\nHighlights:\nWhat we tried, each round-tripped push → API readback → Garmin sync → watch face:\n\n1. `{units: \"bpm\", value, end}` — API accepted; watch showed 214–249 bpm (impossible). FIT export misread `bpm` as percent-of-something.\n2. `{start_bpm, end_bpm}` — watch showed a constant 101, Garmin's \"no valid HR target\" fallback. Schema silently dropped.\n3. Surveyed the Intervals UI target dropdown, concluded \"absolute bpm is impossible\". Wrong — that dropdown picks the units label, not the value shape.\n\nThe breakthrough: build a workout in the Intervals UI with its \"Instant HR\" toggle, then read it back via the API. The corridor's lower bound is `start`, not `value`. That one key name switches the FIT export mode: `{value, end}` routes as a Lap-HR / zone-mapped target (the watch clamps it to its local zones), while `{start, end}` becomes an Instant-HR corridor — the watch displays the raw bpm Intervals computed. Verified end-to-end: Intervals LTHR 172, watch LTHR 168, a 75–82% target rendered 129–141 bpm on the watch — matching Intervals, drift-immune. The fix was a one-word rename after days of probing.\n\nBonus trap from the same session: steps were invisible in the Intervals web UI, because the UI reads a separate top-level `description` in Intervals' text grammar — where `m` means minutes (`200m` = a 200-minute step!), distance must be `mtr`/`km`, a bare `Z2` resolves to power zones even for swims, and a digit at the start of a label gets eaten as a duration. If the parser chokes, Intervals wipes both representations.\n\n...\n\nSteps:\n\n1. Create an event via `POST /api/v1/athlete/{id}/events` with `workout_doc.steps`, where every HR step target is `{\"units\": \"%lthr\", \"start\":, \"end\":}` — never `value`. Percentages, not absolute bpm: Intervals converts at FIT-export time from its own stored threshold.\n2. Set the event's top-level `description` in Intervals' native text grammar so steps render in the web UI, e.g. `- Warm-up easy 10m 75-82% LTHR`. Rules: durations `m`/`s`, distance `mtr`/`km`, blank lines around `Nx` repeat blocks, no digits at the start of labels, no bare `Z ` outside cycling.\n3. Read the event back with `GET /events/{id}`: non-empty `workout_doc.steps` means the parser accepted the description; `steps: []` means it choked.\n4. Sync the watch through Garmin Connect and open the scheduled workout.\n\nVerify: the watch shows a bpm corridor equal to your percentages × the LTHR stored in Intervals — not the watch's own LTHR (easiest to prove when the two differ). The Intervals web UI shows the full step chart instead of just a name and total duration.\n\n---\n\nTitle: API access to Intervals.icu\nURL: https://forum.intervals.icu/t/api-access-to-intervals-icu/609?page=20\nPublished: N/A\nAuthor: N/A\nHighlights:\nYes, Intervals.icu translates fixed power (and other) targets into a range. For power this is only done for outdoor workouts. The default range for power and pace is 2.5%. For HR it is 1.5%. If the athlete has changed Garmin stuff in /settings then these are the defaults:\n\nYou can also supply powerRange, hrRange and paceRange query parameters to the call (e.g. `powerRange=5.0`) for 5%. Use 0.0 to disable range. I am going to update the docs.\n\n...\n\nI have added GET /api/v1/athlete/{id}/activity-hr-curves{ext}\n\n---\n\nTitle: Events created via API with `workout_doc`: the web download produces an empty FIT while the API download does not — which representation feeds device sync? - Questions & Help - Intervals.icu Forum\nURL: https://forum.intervals.icu/t/events-created-via-api-with-workout-doc-the-web-download-produces-an-empty-fit-while-the-api-download-does-not-which-representation-feeds-device-sync/132326\nPublished: 2026-09-08T00:00:00.000Z\nAuthor: Tomas_Povina, R2Tom, MedTechCD\nHighlights:\n## 1. The same event exports differently through the web and through the API\n\nOne of our pushed events (`Ritmo Suave`, a single 10 km step):\n\n| download path | file size | `workout_step` messages | `num_valid_steps` |\n\n|—|—|—|—|\n\n| API, `GET /events?category=WORKOUT&ext=fit` | **119 bytes** | **1** | **1** |\n\n| Web UI download button | **92 bytes** | **0** | **0** |\n\nSame event, same day, no edits in between. The web download was repeated after a full re-upsert\n\nof every event, so this isn’t a stale-cache effect. `GET` on the event returns\n\n`workout_doc.steps` intact either way.\n\n## 2. A controlled A/B pair isolates the cause to the *vehicle*\n\nTwo events describing the **same** workout (10 km @ 5:30/km), differing only in how it was sent:\n\n| control | payload sent | `workout_doc` after GET | FIT via **API** | FIT via **web** |\n\n|—|—|—|—|—|\n\n| **A** | `description: “- 10km 5:30/km Pace”` only | 1 step (compiled by Intervals) | 121 b / **1 step** | 121 b / **1 step** |\n\n| **B** | `workout_doc` only, no description | 1 step (exactly as sent) | 119 b / **1 step** | 92 b / **0 steps** |\n\nA workout authored as **text** exports correctly through both paths. One authored as\n\n**`workout_doc`** exports correctly only through the API — the web generator appears not to see\n\nsteps that were uploaded as `workout_doc` rather than compiled from the description text.\n\nThis lines up with [#124215]( API: Create or update workout without using description text syntax? ),\n\nwhere a user created a workout via `workout_doc`, got well-formed steps back from the backend, and\n\nstill found “the pace range is missing” once it reached Garmin — an independent report on a real\n\ndevice rather than on a download.\n\n## 3. Pace targets need the athlete’s threshold pace — solved, but worth documenting\n\nInitially the exported step came out with no target at all:\n\n```\n\nthreshold\\_pace: null → 92 bytes step 0: duration = DISTANCE 1000000 (10 km, cm) target = OPEN\n\n```\n\nThe pace was present in the compiled `workout_doc` the whole time —\n\n`{“pace”:{“units”:“secs/km”,“value”:330},“distance”:10000,“duration”:3300}`, and Intervals even\n\nderived the 3300 s duration from it — but it never reached the FIT.\n\nFollowing [#130706]( Pace targets lost in Garmin export for API-created running workouts — steps arrive on watch as \"No Target\" (parsed correctly in workout_doc) ),\n\nwe set a Run threshold pace in the athlete’s sport settings and re-exported the same event:\n\n```\n\nthreshold\\_pace set → 146 bytes step 0: duration = DISTANCE 1000000 target = SPEED 2.955–3.106 m/s\n\n```\n\nThat’s a 5:22–5:38 /km band around the 5:30 we prescribed, so a single prescribed value is widened\n\nby roughly ±8 s/km on export — good to know, and it confirmed for us that we should keep emitting\n\nsingle values rather than bands.\n\nSo this one is a **configuration prerequisite** , not a bug. It’s just an invisible one from an\n\nintegrator’s side: an unset threshold silently strips targets from otherwise valid workouts, and\n\nnothing in the API response hints at it. A line in the upload guide would save the next person the\n\nsame hunt.\n\n...\n\nWe followed your advice and switched our emitter to the description field:\nsame workout that exported as 92 bytes / 0 steps now comes out as 450 bytes\nwith all 8 steps and proper speed targets. The preflight idea (checking the\nathlete’s zones before syncing) is implemented too.\n\n---\n\nTitle: HR target workouts broken on Edge 840 firmware ≥30.18 — interpreted as %HRmax instead of bpm - Bug Reports - Intervals.icu Forum\nURL: https://forum.intervals.icu/t/hr-target-workouts-broken-on-edge-840-firmware-30-18-interpreted-as-hrmax-instead-of-bpm/130090\nPublished: 2026-05-19T00:00:00.000Z\nAuthor: N/A\nHighlights:\n## Summary\n\nAfter installing Edge 840 firmware 30.18 (changelog entry: “Aligned presentation of time in zones for power and heart rate with Garmin Connect”),\nstructured workouts pushed via the intervals.icu API with HR-bpm targets are misinterpreted by the device.\nThe Edge now displays the workout’s lower HR bound as `bpm_value × HRmax / 100`.\nFor an athlete with HR max 199 and a target of 122 bpm, the Edge alarms `\"HR below minimum 242 bpm\"` (= 122 % × 199 ≈ 242.8) continuously throughout the ride.\n\nThe same workout structure worked correctly on the Edge before the firmware update.\n\n## Reproduction\n\nPush a Z2 workout with HR-bpm target via `/events/bulk`:\n\n```bash\ncurl -X POST 'https://intervals.icu/api/v1/athlete/0/events/bulk?upsert=true&upsertOnUid=false&updatePlanApplied=false' \\\n  -u \"API_KEY:$KEY\" -H \"Content-Type: application/json\" -d '[{\n    \"external_id\": \"hr-bug-repro\",\n    \"category\": \"WORKOUT\",\n    \"type\": \"Ride\",\n    \"start_date_local\": \"2026-05-25T09:00:00\",\n    \"name\": \"HR bug repro\",\n    \"moving_time\": 3600,\n    \"description\": \"- 60m 122-147bpm Z2 endurance\",\n    \"workout_doc\": {\n      \"target\": \"HR\",\n      \"steps\": [{\n        \"intensity\": \"active\",\n        \"duration\": 3600,\n        \"hr\": {\"start\": 122, \"end\": 147, \"units\": \"bpm\"},\n        \"text\": \"Z2 endurance\"\n      }]\n    }\n  }]'\n\n```\n\nLoad the synced workout on an Edge 840 running firmware ≥30.18 and start the activity. The device alarms `\"HR below minimum 242 bpm\"` (where 242 = `122 × HRmax / 100`).\n\n## Investigation findings\n\nWhile debugging this on my own account, I observed that all public export endpoints strip HR-bpm targets entirely:\n\n| Export | HR-bpm result | Power-%FTP result (control) |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| `/events/{id}/download.zwo` | `` — no target attrs | `` |\n| `/events/{id}/download.mrc` | Empty `[COURSE DATA]` | Populated with target % values |\n| `/events/{id}/download.fit` | 195 bytes, no target zone | 204 bytes, with power target |\n\nThe `hr_zone` variant (`hr: {units: \"hr_zone\", value: 2}`) also strips on export — even though intervals.icu maintains a distinct HR-zone internal model (`zoneTimes` returns Z1–Z5 instead of the power-zone Z1–Z7 when `target: HR`).\n\nAdditionally, intervals.icu’s own Zwift importer drops HR targets : uploading a `.zwo` containing `` via `/folders/{id}/import-workout` results in a stored `workout_doc` where the step is replaced by `power: {units: \"%ftp\", value: 0}` — the HR target metadata is discarded entirely.\n\nThese observations suggest the `workout_doc` HR-target representation either has no Garmin-format translation in your codebase, or the internal Garmin Connect sync API path emits HR values without an explicit absolute-bpm unit marker — relying on a pre-30.18 lenient Edge default that no longer exists.\n\n## Suspected cause\n\nintervals.icu’s Garmin Connect sync emits HR target values without explicitly tagging them as absolute bpm. Pre-30.18, the Edge defaulted to “treat as bpm.” Post-30.18, the Edge follows Garmin Connect’s documented convention and defaults to “treat as %HRmax.” The numeric value passes through unchanged, but its meaning flips.\n\n## Workaround (for affected users)\n\nSwitch HR-target workouts to power-target with `{units: \"%ftp\"}` ranges — these survive both the export pipeline and the Garmin Connect sync correctly. For HR-ceiling enforcement on the Edge, use the device-level HR alert (Activity Profile → Alerts → Heart Rate → set ceiling), which works regardless of workout target.\n\n---\n\nTitle: Syncing Pace & HR targets to Garmin - Intervals.icu Forum\nURL: https://forum.intervals.icu/t/syncing-pace-hr-targets-to-garmin/130238\nPublished: 2026-06-01T00:00:00.000Z\nAuthor: N/A\nHighlights:\nIntervals hasn’t figured out yet how to send workouts over the Garmin API with multiple targets.\nLet’s be clear: Intervals is NOT sending FIT workouts to Garmin because Garmin doesn’t allow that. Intervals sends a workout in a script format to the Garmin API and Garmin Connect translates that to a workout on your device. The target type definition is not clear or Garmin is somehow blocking that.\nThis is kind of a hot topic over the last couple of months but the solution hasn’t been found yet.\n\n---\n\nTitle: Garmin cuts off INTERVALS and other third-party apps\nURL: https://the5krunner.com/2026/10/14/garmin-api-intervals-third-party-apps/\nPublished: 2026-10-14T00:00:00.000Z\nAuthor: N/A\nHighlights:\nApps built on larger, better-known platforms such as Intervals.icu are also in the firing line.\n\n## Why Intervals Pro closed\n\nQuick Navigation:Toggle\n\nThe terms will stop Intervals.icu from passing Garmin data to services Garmin hasn’t approved, like Intervals Pro but there are many others. The app closed on 12 October rather than wait for the data it coached from to disappear.\n\n“ Intervals.pro is closing because Garmin are going to restrict downstream data, just as Strava have done,” said Max Revitt (owner) talking to this site. “ That’s not a battle I’m willing to fight.” Intervals Pro was an independent app with no link to that platform beyond reading athletes’ data from it. Its closure notice says unused prepaid time is refunded automatically and athletes can download their coaching conversations until 19 October. Workouts already on athletes’ training calendars stay there.\n\n## Going Forward: What athletes might lose and what still works\n\n- You lose this: Apps that pull your Garmin data through another platform will likely lose access to it all, including past activities.\n- This still works: the platform you sync to directly from Garmin Connect still receives your data\n- This still works: data from other watch brands is unaffected.\n\nThe developer of one affected platform expects the restriction to cover entire activities, FIT files, data streams, and wellness data, including already stored history. It applies only to data that arrives through Garmin’s API, so a FIT file you upload yourself should stay available to connected apps. Data from COROS, Polar, Suunto and Oura stays available under each brand’s own terms, at least for now. There is no date for the change yet, and platforms will need time to make it.\n\n...\n\nWill my training platform still show my Garmin activities?\nYes, if it receives Garmin data directly. The restriction applies to apps that read your data from that platform rather than directly from Garmin.\n\nCan I still export my own Garmin data?\nYes. Garmin Connect still lets you download your FIT files, and files you upload yourself are expected to stay outside the restriction.\n\nWhen does the change take effect?\nGarmin hasn’t announced a date, and platforms will need time to make the change.\n\nWhy did Intervals Pro close?\nIntervals Pro read athletes’ Garmin data through a third-party platform. Garmin’s new terms will end that access, so the app closed on 12 October 2026 rather than wait.\n\n---\n\nTitle: Pace targets lost in Garmin export for API-created running workouts — steps arrive on watch as \"No Target\" (parsed correctly in workout_doc) - Intervals.icu Forum\nURL: https://forum.intervals.icu/t/pace-targets-lost-in-garmin-export-for-api-created-running-workouts-steps-arrive-on-watch-as-no-target-parsed-correctly-in-workout-doc/130706\nPublished: 2026-07-17T00:00:00.000Z\nAuthor: N/A\nHighlights:\nSetup\n\n- Workouts created via the API: `POST /api/v1/athlete/0/events/bulk?upsert=true`, `category: WORKOUT`, `type: Run`, description in the plain-text workout format, `external_id` set\n- Athlete settings: `garmin_pace_range = 2.5` (default), Garmin Connect linked with “Upload planned workouts” enabled\n- Watch: Garmin Forerunner [model 165] — happy to provide exact model/firmware\n\nDescription format used (sample):\n\n```auto\n- 2.5km 5:57-5:23/km Pace\n\n```\n\n`- 1.5km 9:06-8:14/km Pace`\n\n`- 2km 6:13-5:37/km Pace`\n\nWhat works (verified via the API):\n\nReading the event back, `workout_doc.steps` is parsed perfectly — every step carries a structured pace range:\n\n```auto\n{\"pace\":{\"start\":357,\"end\":323,\"units\":\"secs/km\"},\"distance\":2500,\"duration\":848}\n\n```\n\n21/21 steps have `pace: {start, end}` on the real workout in question.\n\nWhat fails:\n\nThe workout reaches Garmin Connect (name + description arrive; the Connect app shows the description). On the watch, the steps arrive with correct distances but every step shows “No Target” — no pace target, no alerts during the workout.\n\nWhat we’ve ruled out:\n\n- Syntax: both single-value (`5:40/km Pace`) and range (`5:45-5:35/km Pace`) formats parse into correct `workout_doc` pace objects; both arrive on the watch as “No Target”\n- Stale Garmin cache: deleting the events and re-creating them fresh (to force a full re-export rather than an update) changes nothing\n- Connect app display quirk (the `[SOLVED]` thread #87358): this is on the watch itself , in the step list and during execution, not just the phone UI\n\n...\n\nSolved — and the cause was on our end, not intervals’. The athlete simply had no Run threshold pace set in their sport settings.\n\nWith threshold_pace unset, intervals drops the per-step pace targets from the Garmin export (it falls back to HR), so every step lands as “No Target” on the watch — even though workout_doc.steps carried correct pace ranges on the intervals side the whole time. HR-target workouts were unaffected because LTHR was set, which is why only pace workouts broke.\n\nFix: set a Run threshold pace — Settings → sport settings, or via API: PUT /api/v1/athlete/0/sport-settings/{runGroupId} with body { “threshold_pace”: \\ }. intervals then auto-populates the pace zones, and after a fresh push the pace targets appear on the watch (confirmed on a Garmin Forerunner).\n\nOne gotcha for anyone re-testing: existing events need a delete + recreate to re-trigger the Garmin export — an in-place update alone doesn’t.\n\n...\n\npointer in the related threads was exactly it.\n\n---\n\nTitle: Power and HR targets for Garmin workouts\nURL: https://forum.intervals.icu/t/power-and-hr-targets-for-garmin-workouts/2926\nPublished: N/A\nAuthor: N/A\nHighlights:\nYou can now specify power and HR targets for workout steps (default is lap power and lap HR):\n\nThis sorts out the issue with HR steps not working on Garmin watches (well at least some of them). You need to choose “Instant HR” (hr=1s in the text):\n\n- warmup 30s 50% LTHR hr=1s\n- Wibble 20m 78% LTHR hr=1s\n- cooldown 30s 40% LTHR hr=1s\n\n...\n\nUnfortunately the target in the Garmin API is just “PACE”. There aren’t other options like there are for power.\n\n---\n\nTitle: heads/main/src/intervals_icu_mcp/workout_syntax.py\nURL: https://github.com/hhopke/intervals-icu-mcp/blob/refs/heads/main/src/intervals_icu_mcp/workout_syntax.py\nPublished: N/A\nAuthor: N/A\nHighlights:\nThis format is\n\n...\n\ntargets for cycling, running, and swimming. Place this text in the 'description'\nfield when creating WORKOUT events via the API.\n\n## Structure Overview\nA workout consists of:\n1. **Title** (first non-empty line, optional when using API - use the 'name' field instead)\n2. **Sections** (Warmup, Main Set, Cooldown, or custom names)\n3. **Steps** within sections (duration + optional target)\n\n```\nSection Name [repeat]\n- duration target\n- duration target\n\nAnother Section\n- duration target\n```\n\n## Sections\nSections group related steps. Common names: `Warmup`, `Warm Up`, `Main Set`, `Main`, `Cooldown`, `Cool Down`.\n\nAdd repeat count after section name:\n\n```\nMain Set 3x\n- 5m 90%\n- 2m 50%\n```\n\nOr standalone repeat:\n\n```\nIntervals 5x\n- 400mtr 110% pace\n- 400mtr Z1\n```\n\n**Important**: Leave a blank line before and after repeat blocks.\n\n...\n\n### Time-Based\n| Format | Example | Description |\n|--------|---------|-------------|\n| `Xm` | `10m` | Minutes |\n| `Xs` | `30s` | Seconds |\n| `Xh` | `1h` | Hours |\n| `XhYm` | `1h30m` | Hours and minutes |\n| `XmYs` | `5m30s` | Minutes and seconds |\n| `X:YY` | `5:00` | Minutes:seconds |\n| `X:YY:ZZ` | `1:30:00` | Hours:minutes:seconds |\n\n### Distance-Based\n| Format | Example | Description |\n|--------|---------|-------------|\n| `Xmtr` | `400mtr` | Meters (swimming/running) |\n| `Xkm` | `5km` | Kilometers |\n| `Xmi` | `3mi` | Miles |\n| `Xyrd` | `100yrd` | Yards (swimming) |\n\n**Important**: Plain `m` always means **minutes**, never meters. For a distance\nin meters use `mtr` (e.g. `400mtr`) — writing `400m` is parsed as 400 minutes.\n\n...\n\n## Target Formats\n\n### Power (Cycling)\n| Format | Example | Description |\n|--------|---------|-------------|\n| `X%` | `90%` | Percentage of FTP |\n| `X% power` | `90% power` | Explicit power target |\n| `X-Y%` | `88-94%` | Power range |\n| `Xw` | `250w` | Absolute watts |\n| `X-Yw` | `240-260w` | Watts range |\n| `ZX` | `Z4` | Power zone (Z1-Z7) |\n\n### Heart Rate\n| Format | Example | Description |\n|--------|---------|-------------|\n| `X% HR` | `75% HR` | Percentage of max HR or LTHR |\n| `X-Y% HR` | `70-80% HR` | HR range |\n| `Xbpm` | `145bpm` | Absolute BPM |\n| `X-Ybpm` | `140-150bpm` | BPM range |\n| `ZX HR` | `Z2 HR` | HR zone |\n\n### Pace (Running/Swimming)\n| Format | Example | Description |\n|--------|---------|-------------|\n| `X% pace` | `90% pace` | Percentage of threshold pace |\n| `X-Y% pace` | `85-90% pace` | Pace range |\n| `X:XX/km pace` | `5:00/km pace` | Absolute pace per kilometer |\n| `X:XX/mi pace` | `8:00/mi pace` | Absolute pace per mile |\n| `X:XX/100m pace` | `1:45/100m pace` | Absolute swim pace per 100 meters |\n| `X:XX/100y pace` | `1:40/100y pace` | Absolute swim pace per 100 yards |\n| `ZX pace` | `Z3 pace` | Pace zone |\n\nThe trailing word `pace` is **required** for absolute pace targets — bare\n`5:00/km` or `1:45/100m` is silently dropped (live-verified). Absolute swim\npace denominators are `/100m` and `/100y` only — note the asymmetry with\ndistance tokens: step distances use `mtr`/`yrd`, but pace denominators use\n`m`/`y` (`/100mtr` and `/100yrd` are silently dropped).\nThreshold pace is expressed *relative* to the sport-settings threshold:\n`100% pace` = run threshold pace / swim CSS (e.g. `- 25m 100% pace`,\n`- 200mtr 100% pace`, range `95-100% pace`). The words `CSS` / `threshold`\nand race-pace vocabulary (`5K pace`, `marathon pace`) are **not** parsed —\na step written that way silently loses its target and contributes no\ntraining load.\n\n...\n\nMultiple targets can be combined on one line:\n```\n- 10m 88-94% 90rpm\n\n...\n\n## Step Type / Intensity\nTag a step's type with `intensity= `, where ` ` is one of `warmup`,\n`active`, `rest`, or `cooldown`. This sets the structured-workout step *type* —\nthe standard warmup/active/rest/cooldown set that devices use to render phases —\nand is stored on the parsed step, but does **not** change its duration, distance,\nzones, or training load.\n\n```\n- 400mtr intensity=warmup free\n- 5m 90% intensity=active\n- 20s intensity=rest\n- 200mtr intensity=cooldown free\n```\n\nOptional: steps under a `Warmup` / `Cooldown` section header are already treated\nas warmup/cooldown — including on device export (FIT), so `intensity=warmup` /\n`intensity=cooldown` add nothing there. The tag that changes device behavior is\n`intensity=rest`: it is the only form that exports a true FIT rest step\n(Garmin/Wahoo render rest steps distinctly). An appended `Ns rest`\n(`- 200mtr Z2 20s rest`) adjusts the planned step duration and load math but\ndoes not export a rest step. Zwift export (.zwo) ignores step types entirely.\nWorks on any sport and step type.\n\n...\n\n### HR Zones (Z1-Z5)\n| Zone | Name | % Max HR |\n|------|------|----------|\n| Z1 | Recovery | 50-60% |\n| Z2 | Aerobic | 60-70% |\n| Z3 | Tempo | 70-80% |\n| Z4 | Threshold | 80-90% |\n| Z5 | VO2max | 90-100% |\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_10","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"mcporter call exa.web_search_exa query='site:forum.intervals.icu/t/ October 2026 \\\"Garmin\\\" \\\"HR\\\" \\\"workout\\\" \\\"range\\\"' numResults=6\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_10","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"mcporter call exa.web_search_exa query='site:forum.intervals.icu/t/ October 2026 \\\"Garmin\\\" \\\"HR\\\" \\\"workout\\\" \\\"range\\\"' numResults=6\"","aggregated_output":"Title: Upload planned workouts to Garmin Connect\nURL: https://forum.intervals.icu/t/upload-planned-workouts-to-garmin-connect/1521?page=2\nPublished: N/A\nAuthor: N/A\nHighlights:\nTx. Some workouts in your library (e.g. “3015”) are missing information due to a bug fixed some time ago. So thats why 3015 didn’t upload to Garmin. I have added code to fix this for new workouts dragged from your library. For workouts already on your calendar you need to make a dummy edit (e.g. just add a space to the description) and then they should upload.\n\n@Aurelien_Decagny @Paavo_Heikkinen I have added outdoor power range and heart rate range settings to the Garmin Connect upload that convert fixed power/HR targets to ranges:\n\nOutdoor power range: Outdoor workout steps with exact power targets are converted into ranges of plus/minus this percentage of the target power.\n\nHeart rate range: Workout steps with exact heart rate targets are converted into ranges of plus/minus this percentage of the target heart rate.\n\n@Aurelien_Decagny Future fitness updates are now done asynchronously so workout edits are much faster.\n\n---\n\nTitle: Custom Heart Rate range planned workout - Training - Intervals.icu Forum\nURL: https://forum.intervals.icu/t/custom-heart-rate-range-planned-workout/72460\nPublished: 2024-07-23T00:00:00.000Z\nAuthor: N/A\nHighlights:\nYou need to specify the HR range in % of threshold HR. This is so workouts are portable between athletes. So if threshold HR is 170 bpm then use 130/170 = 76%. So “- 10m 74-76% HR”.\n\n...\n\nAlso possible with % of maxHR (which could give a slightly wider range of bpm).\n\n...\n\nThank you, I thought about that, just wondering whether absolute values were an option.\nThe question came because in Friel´s book “Fast after 50” there is a heart rate based workout that uses this formula:\nInterval Limits = LTHR - 30 +/- 2\n\nI may also create the workout in Garmin Connect that alows to enter BPM targets.\n\n...\n\nTry not get fixated on an abolute value, as your physiology doesn’t work on on/off switches. It can change day to day, depending on your sleep, hydration, temprature (cooling) and more. Having said that, for the aerobic threshold test/workout that he refers to, limit +/-2bpm, you can use %maxHR, like follows:\n\n-30m 70-71% HR\n\nThis will be 2-3 bpm, maybe 2-4 if the maxHR is low.\nI’m using example values only.\n\nGo ride, and see how you do.\n\nIf testing, it must be the same route, each time, so you have a comparison to the benchmark.\nBut note that power and RPE will fluctuate irrespective of how you feel, due to the wind, elevation, temperature, hydration, sleep all affecting HR.\n\n---\n\nTitle: HR target workouts broken on Edge 840 firmware ≥30.18 — interpreted as %HRmax instead of bpm - Bug Reports - Intervals.icu Forum\nURL: https://forum.intervals.icu/t/hr-target-workouts-broken-on-edge-840-firmware-30-18-interpreted-as-hrmax-instead-of-bpm/130090\nPublished: 2026-05-19T00:00:00.000Z\nAuthor: N/A\nHighlights:\n# HR target workouts broken on Edge 840 firmware ≥30.18 — interpreted as %HRmax instead of bpm\n\n## Summary\n\nAfter installing Edge 840 firmware 30.18 (changelog entry: “Aligned presentation of time in zones for power and heart rate with Garmin Connect”),\nstructured workouts pushed via the intervals.icu API with HR-bpm targets are misinterpreted by the device.\nThe Edge now displays the workout’s lower HR bound as `bpm_value × HRmax / 100`.\nFor an athlete with HR max 199 and a target of 122 bpm, the Edge alarms `\"HR below minimum 242 bpm\"` (= 122 % × 199 ≈ 242.8) continuously throughout the ride.\n\nThe same workout structure worked correctly on the Edge before the firmware update.\n\n...\n\nPush a Z2 workout with HR-bpm target via `/events/bulk`:\n\n```bash\ncurl -X POST 'https://intervals.icu/api/v1/athlete/0/events/bulk?upsert=true&upsertOnUid=false&updatePlanApplied=false' \\\n  -u \"API_KEY:$KEY\" -H \"Content-Type: application/json\" -d '[{\n    \"external_id\": \"hr-bug-repro\",\n    \"category\": \"WORKOUT\",\n    \"type\": \"Ride\",\n    \"start_date_local\": \"2026-05-25T09:00:00\",\n    \"name\": \"HR bug repro\",\n    \"moving_time\": 3600,\n    \"description\": \"- 60m 122-147bpm Z2 endurance\",\n    \"workout_doc\": {\n      \"target\": \"HR\",\n      \"steps\": [{\n        \"intensity\": \"active\",\n        \"duration\": 3600,\n        \"hr\": {\"start\": 122, \"end\": 147, \"units\": \"bpm\"},\n        \"text\": \"Z2 endurance\"\n      }]\n    }\n  }]'\n\n```\n\nLoad the synced workout on an Edge 840 running firmware ≥30.18 and start the activity. The device alarms `\"HR below minimum 242 bpm\"` (where 242 = `122 × HRmax / 100`).\n\n...\n\nWhile debugging this on my own account, I observed that all public export endpoints strip HR-bpm targets entirely:\n\n| Export | HR-bpm result | Power-%FTP result (control) |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| `/events/{id}/download.zwo` | `` — no target attrs | `` |\n| `/events/{id}/download.mrc` | Empty `[COURSE DATA]` | Populated with target % values |\n| `/events/{id}/download.fit` | 195 bytes, no target zone | 204 bytes, with power target |\n\nThe `hr_zone` variant (`hr: {units: \"hr_zone\", value: 2}`) also strips on export — even though intervals.icu maintains a distinct HR-zone internal model (`zoneTimes` returns Z1–Z5 instead of the power-zone Z1–Z7 when `target: HR`).\n\nAdditionally, intervals.icu’s own Zwift importer drops HR targets : uploading a `.zwo` containing `` via `/folders/{id}/import-workout` results in a stored `workout_doc` where the step is replaced by `power: {units: \"%ftp\", value: 0}` — the HR target metadata is discarded entirely.\n\nThese observations suggest the `workout_doc` HR-target representation either has no Garmin-format translation in your codebase, or the internal Garmin Connect sync API path emits HR values without an explicit absolute-bpm unit marker — relying on a pre-30.18 lenient Edge default that no longer exists.\n\n## Suspected cause\n\nintervals.icu’s Garmin Connect sync emits HR target values without explicitly tagging them as absolute bpm. Pre-30.18, the Edge defaulted to “treat as bpm.” Post-30.18, the Edge follows Garmin Connect’s documented convention and defaults to “treat as %HRmax.” The numeric value passes through unchanged, but its meaning flips.\n\n## Workaround (for affected users)\n\nSwitch HR-target workouts to power-target with `{units: \"%ftp\"}` ranges — these survive both the export pipeline and the Garmin Connect sync correctly. For HR-ceiling enforcement on the Edge, use the device-level HR alert (Activity Profile → Alerts → Heart Rate → set ceiling), which works regardless of workout target.\n\n...\n\n> @lukas_klingebiel:\n> \n> `description\": \"- 60m 122-147bpm Z2 endurance\",`\n\nThis is no valid intervals syntax. If you use the API the description should contain valid syntax, this is what will be parsed.\n\nAn example of correct syntax would be:\n\n```auto\n\"description\": \"- 122-147bpm 60m Z2 HR\",\n\n```\n\nBut intervals is not supporting absolute values. The bpm range is considered as text.\n\n---\n\nTitle: Garmin planned workout sync — multi-target steps & zone-only targets - Feature Requests - Intervals.icu Forum\nURL: https://forum.intervals.icu/t/garmin-planned-workout-sync-multi-target-steps-zone-only-targets/130551\nPublished: 2026-06-29T17:42:31.000Z\nAuthor: Il_Jae_Lee\nHighlights:\nTwo related enhancements to how planned workouts sync to Garmin that would make structured sessions translate more faithfully on the watch:\n\n1. Multi-target steps (e.g. HR and Power)\n\nCurrently a workout step syncs with a single target type. It would be great to attach more than one target to the same step — for example a Power primary target with a secondary HR cap/ceiling, or a Power target plus an HR guardrail. This is especially useful for easy/long runs where I want to govern by HR but keep a power ceiling, and for quality work where I want power as the lead with HR as a sanity check. Garmin’s own workout format supports a target plus a secondary/“do not exceed” constraint, so this would mostly be a matter of passing the second target through on sync.\n\n2. Zone-only targets instead of explicit ranges\n\nWhen a step is built against a zone (e.g. “Z2”), it would be ideal to sync it to Garmin as that zone reference rather than expanding it to a fixed min/max range. Two benefits: (a) the watch displays the zone the athlete actually trains by, and (b) if zone boundaries change (FTP/LTHR update), the workout stays correct without rebuilding it. Right now a hardcoded range can drift out of sync with current zones.\n\nBoth would meaningfully improve fidelity between the Intervals.icu calendar and what shows up on the Garmin head unit. Thanks for the great work.\n\n---\n\nTitle: Workout builder\nURL: https://forum.intervals.icu/t/workout-builder/1163?page=53\nPublished: N/A\nAuthor: N/A\nHighlights:\nCheck you are on at least version 2025-10-31 07:40:48 (bottom of /settings page). Also style attributes are not allowed. You can do `foo` but the style is stripped out. For unrecognised tags the text between the tags is kept.\n\n...\n\nAuthor: @Barry_Turner\nPost date: 31 October 2025 10:05 UTC\n\nOkay… its working now.\n\n---\n\nAuthor: @MedTechCD\nPost date: 31 October 2025 11:53 UTC\n\nThat range in Garmin is only implemented to avoid continuous beeping. A fixed value would beep an alarm for every miniscule offset.\n\n...\n\nHi.\n\nCan anyone tell me what the problem is with workouts synchronized with GarminConnect (Edge)?\n\nMany of them (e.g., downloaded from ZwiftInsider) and imported into the workout creator from “Intervals.icu” then synchronize with GarminConnect, and that’s where the problem lies.\n\nGarmin Edge 1040 displays power blocks to the nearest watt (e.g., 165W) rather than a range (e.g., 162-168W).\n\nAs a result, the quality of the training assessment (although technically almost perfect, with a difference of only 1W per block) is often very, very low.\n\nIf I create such a workout “manually” in “intervals.icu” and usually select the option: “Add step/Normal/power %FTP” - then the workout synchronized in this way on GarminEdge displays the expected range (e.g., 162-168W).\n\nTraining with values to the nearest watt and with ranges has an identical entry in the training creator.\n\nI don’t understand 😕\n\nHere is an example:\n\n> - 10m ramp 45%-75%\n> - 2m 60%\n> \n> 4x\n> \n> - 10m 70%\n> - 8m 84%\n> - 8m 60%\n\n...\n\nThe Zwift imported ones are probably “Indoor” workouts by default. Intervals.icu does not convert power values for indoor workouts into ranges. The assumption is that the workout will be used to control an indoor trainer. Check to see if the indoor box is ticked in the workout editor or if the activity type is “Virtual Ride”.\n\n...\n\nDavid, thank you very much.\nUnchecking “Indoor” causes GarminEdge to display the power “range” 🙂\n\n...\n\n## 8. Targets\n\nThere are three main target domains:\n\n- Power\n- Heart rate\n- Pace\n\nPlus combination/range/ramp forms and freeride.\n\n```auto\n<target> ::= <power-target>\n           | <hr-target>\n           | <pace-target>\n           | <zone-target>\n           | <ramp-target>\n           | <freeride-target>\n\n```\n\n### 8.1 Power\n\nPower can be absolute watts, percent of FTP, or ranges of either.\n\n```auto\n<power-target> ::= <power-absolute>\n                 | <power-percent>\n                 | <power-range>\n\n<power-absolute> ::= <number> \"w\"\n\n<power-percent> ::= <number> \"%\"\n\n<power-range> ::= <number> \"-\" <number> \"%\"\n                | <number> \"-\" <number> \"w\"\n\n```\n\nExamples:\n\n```auto\n- 5m 250w\n- 5m 90%\n- 3m 80-90%\n- 4m 220-260w\n\n```\n\n### 8.2 Heart rate\n\nHR as %HR, %LTHR, or by HR zone.\n\n```auto\n<hr-target> ::= <number> \"%\" \"HR\"\n              | <number> \"%\" \"LTHR\"\n              | <zone-token> \"HR\"\n\n<zone-token> ::= \"Z1\" | \"Z2\" | \"Z3\" | \"Z4\" | \"Z5\" | \"Z6\" | \"Z7\"\n\n```\n\nExamples:\n\n```auto\n- 10m 80% HR\n- 10m 95% LTHR\n- 15m Z2 HR\n\n```\n\n### 8.3 Pace\n\nTwo families:\n\n1. Relative: % of threshold pace or pace zone\n2. Absolute pace or pace range\n\n```auto\n<pace-target> ::= <pace-relative>\n                | <pace-absolute>\n                | <pace-absolute-range>\n\n<pace-relative> ::= <number> \"%\" \"Pace\"\n                  | <zone-token> \"Pace\"\n\n<pace-absolute> ::= <pace-time> [<pace-unit>] \"Pace\"\n\n<pace-absolute-range> ::= <pace-time> \"-\" <pace-time> [<pace-unit>] \"Pace\"\n\n<pace-time> ::= <integer> \":\" <two-digit> (* mm:ss *)\n\n<two-digit> ::= <digit> <digit>\n\n<pace-unit> ::= \"/km\"\n              | \"/mi\"\n              | \"/100m\"\n              | \"/500m\"\n              | \"/100y\"\n              | \"/400m\"\n              | \"/250m\"\n\n```\n\nExamples:\n\n```auto\n- 10m 90% Pace\n- 20m Z2 Pace\n- 10m 7:15 Pace\n- 10m 7:15/km Pace\n- 10m 7:15-7:00 Pace\n- 10m 7:15-7:00/km Pace\n\n```\n\n### 8.4 Zone shorthand\n\nZones can be used alone (defaults to a power zone), or qualified for HR/Pace.\n\n```auto\n<zone-target> ::= <zone-token>\n                | <zone-token> \"HR\"\n                | <zone-token> \"Pace\"\n\n```\n\nExamples:\n\n```auto\n- 60m Z2\n- 30m Z3 HR\n- 10m Z1 Pace\n\n```\n\n### 8.5 Ramp\n\nRamp targets increase/decrease over the step:\n\n```auto\n<ramp-target> ::= \"ramp\" <ws> <ramp-range>\n\n<ramp-range> ::= <number> \"-\" <number> \"%\"\n               | <number> \"-\" <number> \"w\"\n\n```\n\nExamples:\n\n```auto\n- 10m ramp 55-75%\n- 8m ramp 45-70%\n- 5m ramp 200-260w\n\n```\n\n---\n\nTitle: Power and HR targets for Garmin workouts\nURL: https://forum.intervals.icu/t/power-and-hr-targets-for-garmin-workouts/2926\nPublished: N/A\nAuthor: N/A\nHighlights:\nYou can now specify power and HR targets for workout steps (default is lap power and lap HR):\n\nThis sorts out the issue with HR steps not working on Garmin watches (well at least some of them). You need to choose “Instant HR” (hr=1s in the text):\n\n- warmup 30s 50% LTHR hr=1s\n- Wibble 20m 78% LTHR hr=1s\n- cooldown 30s 40% LTHR hr=1s\n\n...\n\nDo you plan to add this functionality also to the pace based workouts? By default it is lap average but I’d much rather it would be 3 second average.\n\n...\n\nUnfortunately the target in the Garmin API is just “PACE”. There aren’t other options like there are for power.\n\n...\n\n@david Not sure if anyone else has been successful, but I finally managed to create a structured workout with my Stryd power targets and did it this morning. Below the syntax used, basically the instructions on Garmin during the execution (audio and on watch) were good enough for me to follow the structure. But some tweaks still needed:\n-When displaying the workout on Garmin watch (going in calendar, selecting the workout, then display/preview ... each split I have “Power ... ” prefix and ... the lower target level set, ... the specific power range defined, ... I have when I configure a workout from Stryd and push it to Garmin\n-When uploading the FIT back to Intervals, it has associated correctly the activity to the planned workout… But the intervals, although well synchronized (I had some “Press Lap” splits) the type of intervals are not correct. How to properly force specific intervals to be detected as “Work” and others as “Recovery”? Actually same issue in Garmin Connect when looking at the activity, everything but the cooldown at the end is displayed as Work.\n\nBelow the syntax of the workout (sorry for the french text in-line) - note that I used Google Gemini to get some help to write the structure, but had to iterate a couple of times until it was displaying properly and also looking good on the watch when pushing to Garmin):\n\n```auto\nWarmup\n- Course facile 12m 70-80% FTP\n\nActivation 2x\n- Activation 1m 97-103% FTP\n- Récup 2m 70-80% FTP\n\nTransition\n- Transition 2m 70-80% FTP\n\nMain Set 5x\n- 15s 115-130% FTP\n- Descente 15s Press Lap 65-80% FTP\n- 20s 115-130% FTP\n- Descente 20s Press Lap 65-80% FTP\n- 30s 115-130% FTP\n- Descente 30s Press Lap 65-80% FTP\n- 20s 115-130% FTP\n- Descente 20s Press Lap 65-80% FTP\n- 15s 115-130% FTP\n- Descente 15s Press Lap 65-80% FTP\n\nCooldown\n- Retour au calme 10m 65-80% FTP\n\n```\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
